"""Kenya National Diseases Database - comprehensive crop and livestock diseases"""

DISEASES_DATA = [
  # CROP DISEASES
  {"name":"Maize Lethal Necrosis (MLN)","type":"crop","affects":"Maize","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Yellowing of leaves starting from the edges. Necrosis (browning and dying) of leaf tissue. Premature dying of the whole plant before tasseling. Empty cobs with no grain. Plants look scorched.","causes":"Combined infection by two viruses: Maize Chlorotic Mottle Virus (MCMV) and a potyvirus (SCMV or WSMV). Spread by thrips, aphids, rootworms and contaminated farm tools. Disease spreads very fast.","treatment":"No cure once infected. Remove and destroy infected plants immediately — do NOT compost. Clean all farm tools with bleach. Plant resistant varieties listed by KEPHIS. Keep field weed-free as weeds harbor viruses.","prevention":"Plant MLN-tolerant varieties: DH02, DH04, SEEDCO SC403, H614D. Control thrips and aphids with Actara insecticide. Rotate with non-maize crops. Never plant maize continuously in same field."},

  {"name":"Fall Armyworm (FAW)","type":"crop","affects":"Maize, Sorghum, Wheat, Rice, Vegetables","severity":"high","symptoms":"Ragged holes in leaves. Sawdust-like frass (droppings) in leaf whorl. Worm with inverted Y-shape on head capsule. Caterpillars tunnel into cobs at later stage. Entire field can be devastated in days.","causes":"Moth pest (Spodoptera frugiperda) from Americas. Now established in Kenya. Moths migrate on wind and lay eggs on maize leaves. Caterpillars hatch and feed voraciously. Each female lays 1,000+ eggs.","treatment":"Spray immediately when first damage seen. Effective pesticides: Coragen (chlorantraniliprole) most effective, Belt (flubendiamide), Karate (lambda-cyhalothrin), Ampligo (chlorantraniliprole + lambda-cy). Apply into whorl. Sand + soil + ash into whorl also works.","prevention":"Scout field twice weekly from germination. Spray when 20% plants infested. Use biological control — Bt (Bacillus thuringiensis) spray. Attract natural enemies — plant sunflowers on borders. Early planting before peak moth populations."},

  {"name":"Stalk Borer","type":"crop","affects":"Maize, Sorghum, Sugarcane","severity":"high","symptoms":"Dead heart (dead central shoot) in young plants. Pin holes in leaves forming straight lines. Sawdust frass inside stem. Broken stems at late stage. Pink or white caterpillars found inside stem.","causes":"Pink stalk borer (Sesamia calamistis) or Spotted stalk borer (Chilo partellus). Moths lay eggs on leaf sheaths. Caterpillars bore into stem and feed on growing point.","treatment":"Spray with Karate (lambda-cyhalothrin) or Bulldock (beta-cyfluthrin) at egg hatching stage. Apply granular Furadan into whorl at whorl stage. Remove and destroy dead heart plants.","prevention":"Early planting. Use resistant varieties. Clean field after harvest — remove all crop debris. Biological control using Cotesia parasitoid wasp. Intercrop with push-pull system — silverleaf desmodium repels moths."},

  {"name":"Grey Leaf Spot of Maize","type":"crop","affects":"Maize","severity":"medium","symptoms":"Rectangular grey to tan lesions running parallel to leaf veins. Lesions limited by leaf veins. Pale grey or tan color with yellow halo. Leaves turn completely grey and die from bottom up.","causes":"Fungus Cercospora zeae-maydis. Spreads in humid conditions with morning dew. Cool nights and warm days favor disease. Survives in maize residues on soil surface.","treatment":"Spray with Tilt (propiconazole) or Bumper fungicide. Remove lower leaves to improve airflow. Avoid excessive nitrogen fertilizer.","prevention":"Plant resistant hybrids. Rotate crops — do not plant maize in same field two years running. Plow under or burn crop residues after harvest. Avoid overhead irrigation in evening."},

  {"name":"Maize Streak Virus","type":"crop","affects":"Maize","severity":"high","symptoms":"Narrow broken yellow streaks running along leaf veins. Stunted plant growth. Pale yellow leaves. Streaks start at base of leaf and run to tip. Severely infected plants produce no grain.","causes":"Maize Streak Virus (MSV) transmitted by leafhoppers (Cicadulina species). Leafhopper feeds on infected grass weeds then moves to maize. Cannot spread by contact or tools.","treatment":"No chemical cure. Remove heavily infected plants. Control leafhoppers with Actara (thiamethoxam) or Karate insecticide to reduce spread.","prevention":"Plant virus-resistant varieties. Plant early before leafhopper peak. Keep field borders free of grass weeds that harbor leafhoppers. Destroy volunteer maize between seasons."},

  {"name":"Late Blight (Potato and Tomato)","type":"crop","affects":"Potato, Tomato, Capsicum","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Water-soaked dark brown/black lesions on leaves that enlarge rapidly. White fuzzy mold on underside of leaves in humid weather. Dark brown lesions on stems. Brown rot of tubers/fruits. Entire crop can be destroyed in 3-5 days in wet weather.","causes":"Oomycete pathogen Phytophthora infestans. Spores spread in wind and rain splash. Favored by cool temperatures (15-20°C) and high humidity. Can destroy entire potato/tomato crop in days during rainy season.","treatment":"Spray immediately at first sign. Ridomil Gold (metalaxyl + mancozeb) most effective. Also Acrobat MZ, Profilux, Cymbal, Dithane M45. Spray every 7-10 days during wet season. Remove and destroy infected plant material.","prevention":"Plant resistant varieties: Asante, Kenya Mpya for potato. Prostar F1, Kilele F1 for tomato. Avoid overhead irrigation. Plant in well-drained fields. Spray preventively starting at planting. Certified disease-free seed potato essential."},

  {"name":"Early Blight","type":"crop","affects":"Tomato, Potato, Capsicum, Eggplant","severity":"medium","symptoms":"Dark brown circular spots with concentric rings (target board appearance) on older lower leaves first. Yellow halo around spots. Lesions coalesce causing leaf death. Dark brown stem cankers.","causes":"Fungus Alternaria solani. Spreads by wind and rain splash. Favored by warm temperatures 24-29°C. Survives in plant debris and soil.","treatment":"Spray with Dithane M45 (mancozeb) or Tilt (propiconazole) or Amistar (azoxystrobin). Remove and destroy infected leaves. Apply every 10-14 days.","prevention":"Crop rotation — do not plant same crop in same field. Remove crop debris after harvest. Avoid overhead irrigation. Use clean seed/transplants. Mulch to prevent soil splash."},

  {"name":"Tomato Leaf Curl Virus (TLCV)","type":"crop","affects":"Tomato","severity":"high","symptoms":"Upward curling and cupping of leaves (looks like leaves trying to fold). Yellowing of young leaves. Stunted plants. Reduced fruit set and yield. Very small distorted fruits.","causes":"Tomato Yellow Leaf Curl Virus (TYLCV) spread by silverleaf whitefly (Bemisia tabaci). Whitefly feeds on infected plant then transmits to healthy plants. Disease cannot spread by contact.","treatment":"No chemical cure for infected plant. Control whitefly vector with Actara (thiamethoxam) or Confidor insecticide. Remove and destroy infected plants early.","prevention":"Use whitefly resistant varieties like Kilele F1, Prostar F1. Use yellow sticky traps to monitor and catch whiteflies. Apply reflective mulch — repels whiteflies. Avoid planting near old infected crops. Inspect all transplants before buying."},

  {"name":"Fusarium Wilt of Tomato","type":"crop","affects":"Tomato, Banana, Melon, Beans","severity":"high","symptoms":"Yellowing of leaves starting on one side of plant. Wilting despite adequate watering. Brown discoloration inside stem when cut lengthwise. Death of plant. Roots may be brown and rotten.","causes":"Soil-borne fungus Fusarium oxysporum. Survives many years in soil. Enters through roots. More severe in warm soils above 28°C. Spreads through infected soil, water and tools.","treatment":"No effective chemical treatment once infected. Remove and destroy infected plants. Solarize soil (cover wet soil with clear plastic for 4-6 weeks in sunny season). Do not replant susceptible crops for 3-5 years.","prevention":"Plant resistant varieties. Rotate crops with cereals or legumes. Use raised beds with fresh soil. Disinfect tools with bleach. Graft tomatoes onto resistant rootstock for high value greenhouses."},

  {"name":"Bacterial Wilt of Tomato","type":"crop","affects":"Tomato, Potato, Pepper, Eggplant","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Sudden wilting of entire plant even with moist soil. Plant wilts during day and appears to recover at night initially. No recovery after a few days. When stem is cut and placed in water, milky bacterial ooze is visible. Rapid death.","causes":"Bacterium Ralstonia solanacearum. Soil-borne. Spreads through irrigation water, contaminated soil on tools and shoes. Favored by warm waterlogged soils. Very long survival in soil.","treatment":"No effective chemical treatment. Remove and destroy infected plants immediately. Do not put in compost. Lime soil to raise pH above 7.","prevention":"Plant resistant varieties. Improve field drainage. Do not use overhead irrigation. Rotate with non-solanaceous crops for 3+ years. Disinfect tools and boots. Avoid buying transplants from unknown sources."},

  {"name":"Coffee Berry Disease (CBD)","type":"crop","affects":"Coffee","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Dark sunken lesions on green coffee berries. Berries turn black and fall off before ripening. In severe infections entire crop may be lost. Black mummies (shriveled berries) on tree. Dark lesions on branches.","causes":"Fungus Colletotrichum kahawae. Kenya-specific pathogen. Spreads in wind and rain splash during wet season. Severe in high rainfall areas. Can destroy 80-100% of crop without control.","treatment":"Spray with copper-based fungicides: Kocide, Copper Oxychloride, Champ. First spray at beginning of rains when majority of berries are formed. Continue every 21 days during wet season. 4-6 sprays per season needed.","prevention":"Plant resistant varieties: Ruiru 11, Batian — both CBD resistant and approved by Coffee Board. Prune trees for good air circulation. Avoid shade that keeps trees wet. Clean up fallen berries promptly."},

  {"name":"Coffee Leaf Rust","type":"crop","affects":"Coffee","severity":"high","symptoms":"Yellow-orange powdery spots on underside of leaves. Yellow patches on upper leaf surface. Heavy defoliation of trees. Weakened trees. Reduced next season crop. Orange spores on fingers when touching affected leaves.","causes":"Fungus Hemileia vastatrix. Spreads by wind and rain. Favored by warm humid conditions. Can spread very fast across a region.","treatment":"Spray with Tilt (propiconazole) or Bumper (propiconazole) immediately when first spots seen. Copper-based sprays also work. Apply when new leaf flush appears.","prevention":"Plant rust-resistant varieties: Ruiru 11, Batian are rust resistant. Proper nutrition — healthy trees resist disease better. Prune for good air circulation. Avoid excessive shade."},

  {"name":"Banana Xanthomonas Wilt (BXW)","type":"crop","affects":"Banana — all varieties","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Yellowing and wilting of leaves starting with one or two leaves. Yellow ooze from cut pseudo-stem. Fruits ripen unevenly and rot from inside. Yellow-brown rot of male flower. Entire bunch can rot. Plant eventually dies.","causes":"Bacterium Xanthomonas vasicola pv. musacearum. Spreads by contaminated tools — ONE CUT with infected panga spreads to next plant. Spread by insects visiting infected male buds. Can spread by infected planting material.","treatment":"No chemical cure. Cut and destroy infected plants immediately at soil level. Leave cut stems to dry out in sun — do NOT bury or take off farm. Disinfect all cutting tools in 1:9 bleach solution between plants during any farm work.","prevention":"Buy planting material only from certified clean sources. Disinfect ALL tools before entering banana field — always. Remove male bud (debudding) after bunch forms — stops insect spread. Remove all diseased plants immediately. Do not move banana material from infected areas."},

  {"name":"Black Sigatoka (Banana)","type":"crop","affects":"Banana — all varieties","severity":"high","symptoms":"Black streaks on leaves that enlarge into oval black lesions with yellow halo. Leaves turn yellow then brown and die from tips. Heavy leaf loss reduces bunch size and delays ripening. Fruits ripen prematurely.","causes":"Fungus Mycosphaerella fijiensis. Spreads by wind and rain splash. Most severe in humid high rainfall areas. More aggressive than Yellow Sigatoka.","treatment":"Spray with Tilt (propiconazole), Dithane M45 or Mancozeb fungicide. Remove and destroy infected leaves (leaf trimming/pruning) regularly. Proper spacing to improve air circulation.","prevention":"Plant resistant varieties where available. Ensure good spacing 3x3m. Regular leaf pruning — remove infected leaves before they die. Avoid overhead irrigation. Avoid waterlogged soils."},

  {"name":"Cassava Mosaic Virus (CMD)","type":"crop","affects":"Cassava","severity":"high","symptoms":"Yellow and green mosaic pattern on leaves (patches of yellow and green). Distorted and twisted leaves. Stunted plant growth. Reduced root yield significantly. Sometimes only small underdeveloped roots produced.","causes":"Cassava Mosaic Virus (ACMD) spread by whitefly Bemisia tabaci. Also spread through infected stem cuttings — main method of spread. If you plant infected stems, all plants will be infected.","treatment":"No chemical cure. Remove infected plants. Control whitefly with Actara or Confidor insecticide. Replace with clean planting material from virus-free source.","prevention":"Use clean certified mosaic-free stem cuttings from KALRO or trusted source. Plant resistant varieties: MM96/4271, Tajirika, Karembo. Control whitefly population. Do not take cuttings from infected plants — ever."},

  {"name":"Cassava Brown Streak Virus (CBSV)","type":"crop","affects":"Cassava","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Brown streaks on stems and petioles. Yellow blotches on leaves. Most seriously: brown necrotic rot inside the root that is only visible when cassava is cut. Can look normal outside but completely rotted inside making roots inedible.","causes":"Cassava Brown Streak Virus spread by whitefly and infected cuttings. East African coastal disease spreading inland. Very damaging because root damage is invisible until harvest.","treatment":"No cure. Remove and destroy infected plants. Control whitefly. Plant clean cassava from certified virus-free source.","prevention":"Use certified clean planting material. Plant resistant varieties. Control whitefly strictly. Do not take cuttings from any plant that shows leaf symptoms."},

  {"name":"Sugarcane Smut","type":"crop","affects":"Sugarcane","severity":"high","symptoms":"Long black whip-like structure grows from the growing point of infected stools. Black powdery mass (smut spores) released from whip. Infected plants stunted. Tillers produce multiple thin sick shoots.","causes":"Fungus Sporisorium scitamineum. Spreads through infected ratoon stools and by windblown spores during hot dry weather. Survives in soil and plant debris.","treatment":"Remove and destroy infected stools — do not use for planting. Hot water treatment of seed cane: 50°C for 2 hours kills smut. Spray with Tilt fungicide on emerging shoots.","prevention":"Plant smut-resistant varieties: KCB98-21. Use certified disease-free seed cane only. Remove infected plants promptly before spores spread. Crop rotation in heavily infected fields."},

  {"name":"Tea Blister Blight","type":"crop","affects":"Tea","severity":"high","symptoms":"Small pale green blisters on young expanding leaves. White fungal growth visible in humid conditions. Affected areas turn brown and die. Infected leaves curl and distort. Young shoots severely affected. Heavy infection reduces crop yield significantly.","causes":"Fungus Exobasidium vexans. Spreads in cool humid conditions. Most severe at high altitudes above 2000m. Spores spread by wind in moist conditions.","treatment":"Spray with Captafol (where available) or copper-based fungicide. Improve ventilation by pruning surrounding trees. Harvest infected flushes promptly to reduce spore load.","prevention":"Plant resistant clones: SFS 150, BBK 35. Avoid very dense planting. Prune shade trees to reduce humidity. Maintain proper plucking round to remove infected material regularly."},

  {"name":"Pyrethrum Root Rot","type":"crop","affects":"Pyrethrum","severity":"high","symptoms":"Yellowing and wilting of plant. Roots show brown to black rotting. Crown of plant rots at soil level. Plant easily pulled out of ground. Nearby plants also affected.","causes":"Soilborne fungi Pythium and Phytophthora. Caused by waterlogging and poor drainage. More common in heavy rainfall seasons.","treatment":"Remove infected plants. Apply Ridomil fungicide to surrounding plants. Improve field drainage urgently.","prevention":"Plant in well-drained soils only. Avoid waterlogging. Make ridges or raised beds. Rotate with other crops."},

  {"name":"Passion Fruit Woodiness Virus (PWV)","type":"crop","affects":"Passion fruit","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Leaves become small, crinkled and distorted. Fruits develop hard woody thick rind with very little juice inside. Fruits become worthless for market. Plant growth stunted. Entire vine may be lost within 1-2 seasons.","causes":"Passion Fruit Woodiness Virus spread by aphids. Once vine is infected it cannot recover. Spreads quickly through aphid populations in a field.","treatment":"No cure. Remove and destroy infected vines immediately. Control aphids with Actara or Karate insecticide on remaining healthy plants.","prevention":"Plant virus-tolerant variety Frederick. Control aphids strictly — check plants weekly. Remove infected plants immediately — do not keep them. Start new plantings away from old infected plantings."},

  {"name":"Avocado Phytophthora Root Rot","type":"crop","affects":"Avocado","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Leaves turn pale green then yellow, wilt and drop. New growth small and pale. Entire tree declines slowly. Roots are brown and rotted when dug up. Small feeder roots completely destroyed. Tree eventually dies.","causes":"Water mold Phytophthora cinnamomi. Lives in soil permanently once established. Spreads through waterlogged conditions, infected soil on tools and shoes, and irrigation water from infected areas.","treatment":"Apply Ridomil or Aliette fungicide as soil drench to root zone twice yearly. Improve drainage urgently. Fertilize healthy-looking trees to boost vigor. Cannot cure severely infected trees.","prevention":"Plant on well-drained slopes or raised mounds. Never plant in waterlogged areas. Fuerte and G6 are more tolerant than Hass. Clean tools before entering orchard. Monitor irrigation carefully."},

  {"name":"Mango Anthracnose","type":"crop","affects":"Mango, Avocado, Papaya","severity":"high","symptoms":"Black irregular spots on leaves. Flower clusters turn black and die (blossom blight) — major cause of crop loss. Green fruits develop dark spots and rot before ripening. Post-harvest rot: brown-black rot develops on stored fruits.","causes":"Fungus Colletotrichum gloeosporioides. Spores spread by rain splash and wind. Most damaging at flowering and fruit set stage. Worst during wet weather.","treatment":"Spray at bud break and every 2-3 weeks during flowering and fruit set. Use Dithane M45, Ridomil, Amistar (azoxystrobin) or Score (difenoconazole). Post-harvest: hot water dip 52°C for 5 minutes reduces storage rot.","prevention":"Prune trees for good air circulation after harvest. Remove and destroy infected plant parts. Spray preventively before flower bud break. Apply wax coating to harvested fruits for storage."},

  {"name":"Mango Fruit Fly","type":"crop","affects":"Mango, Tomato, Papaya, Citrus","severity":"high","symptoms":"Small holes in fruit skin where female laid eggs. Maggots (larvae) inside fruit causing internal rot. Premature fruit drop. Infested fruits have liquid oozing from holes. Entire fruit rots from inside.","causes":"Ceratitis cosyra (Mango fruit fly) and Bactrocera dorsalis (Oriental fruit fly). Female flies puncture ripening fruit to lay eggs. Maggots hatch and feed inside destroying fruit quality.","treatment":"Bait sprays: protein hydrolysate + Malathion insecticide applied to parts of tree canopy — not whole tree. Collect and destroy fallen infested fruits daily. Male annihilation technique with methyl eugenol traps.","prevention":"Harvest at mature green stage before flies attack ripening fruit. Bag individual fruits on tree. Install pheromone traps around orchard. Protein bait stations. Good farm sanitation — collect all fallen fruits."},

  {"name":"Citrus Greening Disease (HLB)","type":"crop","affects":"All citrus — orange, lemon, lime, tangerine","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Yellow shoots on one part of tree (blotchy mottle). Asymmetric yellowing of leaves unlike nutrient deficiency which is uniform. Small lopsided bitter fruits that do not ripen properly. Green color remains at bottom of fruit. Bitter tasteless juice. Tree gradually declines and dies over years.","causes":"Bacterium Candidatus Liberibacter asiaticus spread by Asian Citrus Psyllid insect. No cure exists. Disease is spreading across Kenya and Africa.","treatment":"No cure. Remove and destroy infected trees to prevent spread. Control psyllid vector very aggressively with Actara, Confidor or Karate insecticide. Report suspected cases to county agriculture office.","prevention":"Buy certified disease-free grafted nursery trees only. Control psyllid insects — the insect vector. Quarantine new trees before planting near existing orchard. Inspect trees regularly for psyllid nymphs on new growth."},

  {"name":"Rose Powdery Mildew","type":"crop","affects":"Roses, Beans, Peas, Squash","severity":"high","symptoms":"White powdery coating on young leaves, shoots and buds. Distorted and curled new growth. Infected buds fail to open properly. Affected leaves turn yellow and drop. Reduces flower quality and export value significantly.","causes":"Fungus Podosphaera pannosa (roses) or Erysiphe species (vegetables). Spreads by wind-borne spores. Favored by warm dry days and cool humid nights — typical Kenya highland conditions.","treatment":"Spray with Tilt (propiconazole), Bumper, Topas, sulfur-based fungicides or potassium bicarbonate. Spray every 7-10 days. Ensure thorough coverage of undersides of leaves and new growth.","prevention":"Grow resistant varieties. Avoid excessive nitrogen fertilizer. Improve air circulation by proper spacing and pruning. Avoid wetting foliage during irrigation."},

  {"name":"Botrytis Grey Mould","type":"crop","affects":"Roses, Strawberry, Tomato, French Beans, Carnations","severity":"high","symptoms":"Grey fuzzy mold on flowers, fruits and stems. Soft brown rot of affected tissue. Flowers fail to open and turn brown. Rose petals develop small brown spots (Botrytis spotting). Rapid spread in cool humid conditions.","causes":"Fungus Botrytis cinerea. Spreads by spores in humid air. Most severe in cool temperatures 15-20°C and relative humidity above 90%. Very common in Kenyan highlands.","treatment":"Spray with Switch (cyprodinil + fludioxonil), Rovral (iprodione), or Scala (pyrimethanil). Remove infected plant material immediately. Improve ventilation urgently.","prevention":"Ensure excellent air circulation in greenhouse or tunnels. Remove dead plant material promptly. Avoid wetting flowers. Reduce humidity with ventilation and heating at night. Rotate with different fungicide groups to prevent resistance."},

  {"name":"Club Root of Brassicas","type":"crop","affects":"Cabbage, Kale, Cauliflower, Broccoli","severity":"high","symptoms":"Plants wilt during hot part of day but partially recover at night. Stunted yellowing plants. Roots show grotesque club-shaped swellings and distortions. Affected roots cannot absorb water and nutrients properly. Whole crop may fail.","causes":"Soil pathogen Plasmodiophora brassicae. Survives in soil for 10-20+ years once present. Spreads through infected soil, water, boots and tools. Acid soils make disease much worse.","treatment":"No effective chemical treatment. Remove infected plants. Raise soil pH to 7.2+ using lime — reduces severity. Apply calcium cyanamide fertilizer as soil treatment.","prevention":"Raise soil pH by applying agricultural lime — most important prevention. Long rotation away from brassicas (minimum 5 years). Use clubroot-resistant varieties. Clean boots and tools before entering field. Buy transplants only from certified clean sources."},

  {"name":"Onion Purple Blotch","type":"crop","affects":"Onion, Leek, Garlic","severity":"high","symptoms":"Water-soaked lesions that turn brown then purple with yellow margins. Lesions develop brown to dark purple fuzzy mold in humid conditions. Leaf tips die back. Severe infections kill entire leaves reducing bulb size.","causes":"Fungus Alternaria porri. Spreads by wind and rain. Favored by warm temperatures 21-30°C and leaf wetness from rain or dew. Most common in wet season.","treatment":"Spray with Dithane M45 or Tilt (propiconazole) every 7-10 days during wet season. Remove badly infected leaves. Avoid overhead irrigation in evenings.","prevention":"Ensure good plant spacing for air circulation. Avoid overhead irrigation. Do not irrigate in late afternoon or evening. Crop rotation with non-allium crops."},

  {"name":"Bean Angular Leaf Spot","type":"crop","affects":"Common Beans, French Beans, Cowpeas","severity":"high","symptoms":"Angular reddish-brown spots on leaves limited by leaf veins — characteristic square or angular shape. Spots turn dark brown with grey centers. Infected seeds show brown blotches. Seed pods develop dark brown spots.","causes":"Fungus Phaeoisariopsis griseola. Spreads through infected seeds and rain splash. Survives on infected crop debris. More severe in cool wet weather.","treatment":"Spray with Dithane M45 or copper-based fungicide. Start at flowering stage. Repeat every 10-14 days. Remove and destroy severely infected plants.","prevention":"Use certified clean disease-free seed. Plant resistant varieties like Mwitemania. Rotate crops. Remove crop debris after harvest. Avoid overhead irrigation."},

  {"name":"Wheat Yellow Rust","type":"crop","affects":"Wheat, Barley","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Bright yellow powdery stripes running along leaf veins — very distinctive yellow color. Yellow pustules (blisters) in parallel stripes. Infected leaves turn yellow and die. Severe infections kill entire crop. Can spread across thousands of hectares very rapidly.","causes":"Fungus Puccinia striiformis. Spreads by wind-blown spores across hundreds of kilometers. Favored by cool temperatures 10-15°C and leaf wetness. Can destroy entire wheat belts in a season.","treatment":"Spray immediately at first sign — do not delay. Use Tilt (propiconazole), Bumper or Folicur (tebuconazole). One spray may stop epidemic if applied early. Report outbreak to Kenya Plant Health Inspectorate Service (KEPHIS).","prevention":"Plant resistant varieties: Kenya Fahari, Kenya Swara. Monitor crops twice weekly during cool wet weather. Early planting sometimes avoids peak rust pressure. Good crop nutrition helps plant withstand disease better."},

  {"name":"Rice Blast","type":"crop","affects":"Rice","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Diamond-shaped lesions with grey center and brown margin on leaves (leaf blast). Neck of panicle rots and turns grey-brown causing empty grains (neck blast — most damaging). Water-soaked lesions on stems (node blast). Can destroy up to 100% of rice grain in severe outbreaks.","causes":"Fungus Pyricularia oryzae. Spreads by windborne spores. Favored by heavy dew, fog, cool nights (below 20°C) and warm days. High nitrogen fertilizer increases susceptibility.","treatment":"Spray with Tricyclazole (Beam) or Isoprothiolane (Fuji-One) at first sign of leaf blast. Treat neck blast before and after heading — most critical timing.","prevention":"Plant blast-resistant varieties: Komboka. Avoid excessive nitrogen fertilizer. Drain field periodically. Ensure good air circulation with proper spacing. Treat seeds with fungicide before planting."},

  {"name":"Potato Bacterial Wilt","type":"crop","affects":"Potato, Tomato, Eggplant, Pepper","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Sudden wilting of plant in daytime even when soil is moist. Entire plant wilts rapidly — more sudden than other wilts. When stem is cut and held in clean water, milky bacterial ooze streams out — diagnostic test. Potato tubers show brown discoloration in vascular ring. Rotten foul-smelling tubers at harvest.","causes":"Bacterium Ralstonia solanacearum. Soil-borne pathogen. Survives 3-5 years in soil. Spreads through irrigation water, contaminated soil on tools, boots and vehicles.","treatment":"No effective chemical treatment available. Remove and destroy infected plants. Do not compost infected material. Apply agricultural lime to raise soil pH. Long rotation away from susceptible crops.","prevention":"Plant certified clean seed potato from KEPHIS-approved source. Good field drainage — avoid waterlogging. Rotate with cereals for minimum 3 years. Disinfect tools with 10% bleach solution. Do not move soil from infected fields."},

  # LIVESTOCK DISEASES
  {"name":"East Coast Fever (ECF)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Fever above 39.5°C. Swelling of lymph nodes especially around ears and behind shoulders. Labored breathing — cattle breathe with mouth open. Runny eyes and nose. Loss of appetite. Animals stand in shade. Death within 2-3 weeks if untreated.","causes":"Parasite Theileria parva transmitted by brown ear tick (Rhipicephalus appendiculatus). Tick attaches and injects parasite. Disease attacks white blood cells (lymphocytes) and can be fatal. Very common in highland tick areas.","treatment":"Buparvaquone (Butalex) injection — must be given early and at correct dose. Parvaquone (Clexon) also works. Treat with anti-fever drugs (flunixin). Supportive care — good feeding, water, shade. Treatment expensive but saves valuable animals.","prevention":"Immunization (ITM — Infection and Treatment Method) using live parasites plus Terramycin — provides lasting protection. Tick control: dip or spray cattle every week with acaricide (Triatix, Bayticol, Deadline) in high ECF areas. Avoid moving cattle from clean areas to tick-infested areas without immunization first."},

  {"name":"Foot and Mouth Disease (FMD)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle, Sheep, Goats, Pigs","severity":"critical","symptoms":"High fever suddenly. Blisters (vesicles) on tongue, gums, lips and between hooves. Excessive salivation — cattle salivate and foam at mouth. Animals very lame — unwilling to walk. Blisters burst leaving painful ulcers. Milk production drops dramatically. In young animals can cause sudden death from heart damage.","causes":"Foot and Mouth Disease Virus (FMDV) — 7 types exist in Africa. Spreads extremely rapidly by direct contact, contaminated air, vehicles, clothing and equipment. Highly contagious — one infected animal can infect hundreds. Not dangerous to humans.","treatment":"No specific treatment. Anti-fever drugs for comfort. Antibiotic ointment on ulcers to prevent secondary infection. Soft feeds and good water access. Most adult animals recover in 2-3 weeks but milk production loss is permanent for that lactation.","prevention":"Vaccination every 6 months — essential in Kenya. Quarantine any suspected cases immediately. Report to Veterinary Authority — it is a notifiable disease. Restrict movement of animals from outbreak areas. Disinfect vehicles and equipment with citric acid."},

  {"name":"Brucellosis (Undulant Fever)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle, Goats, Sheep, Pigs — ALSO DANGEROUS TO HUMANS","severity":"critical","symptoms":"In female animals: abortion in last 3 months of pregnancy — most obvious sign. Retained placenta after abortion. Reduced milk production. Bulls: orchitis (swollen testicles) and reduced fertility. In HUMANS who drink raw milk: recurring fever, joint pains, headache and weakness — can be debilitating for years.","causes":"Bacteria Brucella abortus (cattle), Brucella melitensis (goats/sheep), Brucella suis (pigs). Spreads by contact with aborted fetus, birth fluids and placenta of infected animals. HUMANS GET INFECTED BY DRINKING RAW MILK OR HANDLING ABORTIONS — always boil milk!","treatment":"Antibiotics: Oxytetracycline long course in animals. But treatment often not effective. Humans: long course of doxycycline and rifampin. Prevention by vaccination much better than treatment. Infected animals should be culled.","prevention":"Vaccinate all female calves at 4-8 months with RB51 vaccine — one dose gives lifetime protection. Do NOT vaccinate adults. ALWAYS boil milk before drinking. Wear gloves when assisting births and handling abortions. Test and cull positive animals. Brucellosis is a zoonosis — deadly serious public health issue."},

  {"name":"Lumpy Skin Disease (LSD)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle","severity":"high","symptoms":"Firm raised lumps (nodules) on skin especially on neck, back and legs. Lumps 2-5cm diameter. Fever. Swelling of lymph nodes. Reduced milk production. Limping. Swelling of legs and brisket. Lumps later become necrotic (die and form craters). Severely affected animals may die.","causes":"Lumpy Skin Disease Virus — related to sheep pox. Spread by blood-sucking insects: mosquitoes, flies, stable flies. Can also spread by contaminated needles and equipment. More severe in areas with high insect populations.","treatment":"No specific antiviral treatment. Anti-fever drugs (flunixin) reduce discomfort. Antibiotic injections to prevent secondary bacterial infection in ulcers. Wound dressing on necrotic lumps. Supportive care — good feed and water.","prevention":"Annual vaccination with Lumpy Skin Disease vaccine — very effective. Control biting insects with pour-on insecticides and insect repellents. Move cattle away from waterlogged areas with high insect numbers. Quarantine new animals before mixing with herd."},

  {"name":"Mastitis (Milk Fever - Udder Infection)","type":"livestock","affects":"Dairy Cattle, Goats, Sheep","severity":"high","symptoms":"Hot, swollen, hard and painful udder or one or more quarters. Milk is abnormal: watery, yellowish, clotted, blood-tinged or has flakes. Reduced milk production. Cow reluctant to let down milk. Kicking during milking. Fever in acute cases. In severe cases quarter may be permanently damaged.","causes":"Bacteria entering teat canal during and after milking. Most common: Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus species, E. coli, Klebsiella. Spread by contaminated milking hands, teat cups, flies, and from cow to cow via same milking cloths.","treatment":"Strip all milk from affected quarter frequently throughout day. Inject antibiotic intramammary tubes (Mastiplan, Pentrexyl, Lincomycin) into teat canal after milking — continue 3-5 days. Inject systemic antibiotics in severe cases. Teat dipping after every milking. Anti-inflammatory drugs for severe swelling.","prevention":"Fore-strip before milking — check for clots. Dip teats in iodine-based teat dip after EVERY milking. Use separate clean cloth per cow. Dry cow antibiotic therapy at end of lactation — very important. Keep cubicles and housing clean and dry. California Mastitis Test monthly to detect subclinical mastitis early."},

  {"name":"Newcastle Disease (NCD)","type":"livestock","affects":"All poultry — chickens, turkeys, ducks, guinea fowl","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Sudden death of large numbers of birds. Surviving birds: twisted necks (torticollis — head twisted sideways or backwards), inability to walk or stand. Respiratory signs: gasping, sneezing, rattling sound. Green watery diarrhea. Complete loss of egg production. Swelling of head and wattles. Mortality can reach 100% in unvaccinated flocks.","causes":"Newcastle Disease Virus (NDV). Extremely contagious. Spreads by respiratory secretions, droppings, contaminated feed and equipment. Spreads very fast through a flock. Wild birds can be carriers. Can survive in environment for weeks. No treatment exists.","treatment":"No specific treatment or cure. Antibiotics do not help (virus disease). Supportive care: vitamins in water, extra heat, reduce stress. The best response is good vaccination program BEFORE outbreak.","prevention":"VACCINATION IS ESSENTIAL — this is the most important rule in poultry farming. Lasota vaccine in eye drop or drinking water at 7 days, 28 days, then monthly. Killed vaccine injection before laying. Good biosecurity: no visitors in poultry house, clean and disinfect. Report suspected outbreak to Veterinary Authority."},

  {"name":"Gumboro Disease (IBD)","type":"livestock","affects":"Chickens 3-6 weeks old — most common","severity":"high","symptoms":"Sudden depression — birds huddle together and are sleepy. White watery diarrhea. Birds peck at their own vent area. Unsteady gait. Ruffled feathers. Death in 3-5 days in severe cases. Burst of deaths then recovery. Survivors have permanently damaged immune system making them susceptible to other diseases.","causes":"Infectious Bursal Disease Virus (IBDV) destroys the bursa of Fabricius — the organ that produces antibodies in young chickens. Extremely hardy virus — survives months in environment and is resistant to many disinfectants.","treatment":"No specific treatment. Vitamins and electrolytes in water. Reduce stress and overcrowding. Anti-diarrhea treatment. Keep warm. Probiotics support gut recovery.","prevention":"Vaccination at 14 days with Gumboro vaccine in drinking water — withhold water 2 hours before. Booster at 28 days. Maternal antibodies from vaccinated parent flocks protect chicks in first 2 weeks. Thorough cleaning and disinfection between batches is critical — the virus is very persistent in litter."},

  {"name":"Coccidiosis (Bloody Droppings)","type":"livestock","affects":"Chickens, Turkeys, Rabbits, Cattle calves","severity":"high","symptoms":"In chickens: bloody red-brown droppings — most obvious sign. Ruffled feathers. Birds huddle together. Weight loss and poor growth. High mortality in young birds especially 2-8 weeks old. In rabbits: bloated stomach, soft droppings, weight loss.","causes":"Single-celled parasites Eimeria species. Very common in all livestock. Chickens: Eimeria tenella (bloody droppings) and other species. Spread by infected droppings contaminating feed and water. Worst in wet, warm, overcrowded conditions.","treatment":"Amprolium (Coccid, Coccistop) in drinking water for 5-7 days. Sulphonamide drugs (Sulphaquinoxaline). Trimethoprim-sulpha combinations. Start treatment early — bloody droppings mean disease well established. Add vitamins K (for bleeding) and A.","prevention":"Coccidiostats in feed: use medicated starter feeds. Good litter management — keep dry and not too deep. Avoid overcrowding. Clean and disinfect houses between batches. For rabbits: raised wire floor prevents contact with droppings. Good hygiene critical."},

  {"name":"Fowl Typhoid / Pullorum","type":"livestock","affects":"Chickens, Turkeys, Guinea Fowl","severity":"high","symptoms":"Sudden deaths in young chicks — dead chicks with no obvious symptoms. Young birds: drowsy, grouped near heat. Whitish-yellow droppings sticking to vent feathers. In adults: dull, pale comb, greenish diarrhea, drop in egg production. Swollen green-colored liver at post-mortem.","causes":"Bacteria Salmonella gallinarum (Fowl Typhoid) and Salmonella pullorum (Pullorum). Highly infectious. Spreads from hen to egg (vertical transmission — infected eggs hatch infected chicks). Also spreads by contaminated feed, water and equipment.","treatment":"Enrofloxacin (Baytril) in drinking water for 5 days. Trimethoprim-sulpha drugs. Neomycin. However, treatment reduces mortality but does not eliminate carrier birds which continue spreading.","prevention":"Buy day-old chicks from Salmonella-free tested breeder flocks and hatcheries. Vaccination with Fowl Typhoid vaccine (where available). Good hygiene — clean and disinfect thoroughly between batches. Rodent control essential as rats are major carriers."},

  {"name":"Avian Influenza (Bird Flu)","type":"livestock","affects":"All birds — chickens, ducks, geese, wild birds","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Sudden mass death of birds — can be 100% mortality overnight. Surviving birds: severe respiratory distress, coughing, sneezing. Swollen blue discolored head, comb and wattles. Green diarrhea. Complete cessation of egg laying. Nervous signs — incoordination. VERY DANGEROUS TO HUMANS — some strains can be fatal to people.","causes":"Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (HPAI) H5N1 and other strains. Spreads by wild birds especially migratory waterfowl. Direct contact, contaminated vehicles, equipment, clothing and shoes. Airborne in confined spaces.","treatment":"No treatment — infected flocks MUST be culled (killed and buried or burned). Mandatory government action. Compensation paid to farmers. Do NOT sell or move birds from suspected outbreak area. REPORT IMMEDIATELY to Veterinary Authority — it is a legally notifiable disease.","prevention":"Strict biosecurity — prevent contact between domestic and wild birds. Net the top of outdoor runs. Control access to poultry houses. Disinfect vehicles, boots and equipment. Report unusual mass deaths immediately. Avoid live bird markets if possible. HIGHLY PATHOGENIC STRAINS CAN KILL HUMANS — seek medical attention after contact with sick birds."},

  {"name":"African Swine Fever (ASF)","type":"livestock","affects":"Pigs — domestic and wild","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Very high fever (40-42°C). Pigs refuse to eat and lie down. Reddish-purple discoloration of skin especially on ears, nose and legs. Abortion in pregnant sows. Death within 5-10 days. Mortality rate 90-100% — almost all infected pigs die. NO TREATMENT EXISTS.","causes":"African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV). Carried by wild warthogs and bush pigs which do not get sick. Spreads to domestic pigs by: Ornithodoros soft tick bites, contact with infected pigs, contaminated pork products, swill (kitchen scraps) containing pork, and fomites (equipment, clothing).","treatment":"NO TREATMENT AND NO VACCINE. Infected pigs must be killed and buried or burned. Do not sell sick pigs — spreading disease is illegal. Contact Veterinary Authority immediately if suspected. Compensation may be available.","prevention":"Keep pigs away from warthogs and bush pigs completely. Never feed pigs raw kitchen waste containing pork or pork products (swill). Never move pigs from infected areas. Disinfect vehicles and equipment. Do not buy pigs from markets during outbreaks. This disease has devastated pig farmers across Africa — prevention is everything."},

  {"name":"PPR - Goat and Sheep Plague","type":"livestock","affects":"Goats, Sheep — can also affect camels","severity":"critical","symptoms":"High fever 40-41°C. Profuse eye discharge — crusty eyes. Thick mucus discharge from nose. Sores in mouth — ulcers on gums and lips. Foul-smelling diarrhea — very characteristic smell. Coughing and pneumonia signs. Death in 5-10 days. Kids and lambs most susceptible — 90-100% mortality in young animals.","causes":"PPR Virus (Peste des Petits Ruminants) — related to cattle rinderpest virus. Spreads by direct contact with infected animals. Secretions from eyes, nose and mouth contaminate surroundings. Highly contagious — spreads rapidly through a flock.","treatment":"No specific antiviral treatment. Supportive care: anti-fever drugs, antibiotic injections to prevent secondary pneumonia, vitamins A and C, oral rehydration salts for diarrhea. Isolate sick animals immediately.","prevention":"Vaccination every 3 years with PPR vaccine — provides good protection. Isolate newly purchased animals for 21 days before mixing with main flock. Quarantine sick animals immediately. Good nutrition helps animals resist disease. Report suspected PPR to Veterinary Authority — it is a notifiable disease."},

  {"name":"Sheep and Goat Enterotoxaemia (Pulpy Kidney)","type":"livestock","affects":"Sheep, Goats — especially young well-fed animals","severity":"high","symptoms":"Sudden death — often the best-looking, fastest-growing animal in flock dies first. Animals found dead without warning. If seen before death: wobbling, convulsions, hyperexcitability then collapse. Diarrhea sometimes seen. Death within hours. Post-mortem: very soft liquid kidneys (pulpy kidney) — characteristic.","causes":"Bacteria Clostridium perfringens type D (sheep) or type C (goats). These bacteria live normally in gut but multiply explosively when animal suddenly eats lush green pasture or too much concentrate feed. Toxins produced are fatal very rapidly.","treatment":"Very rarely saves animal as death is so rapid. Antitoxin injection may help very early cases. Reduce feed and move off lush pasture immediately.","prevention":"Vaccination before lush pasture season — most important step. Vaccinate annual plus before lambing or kidding. Gradual change from dry to lush pasture — do not suddenly move animals to rich green pasture. Avoid sudden changes in feed type."},

  {"name":"Foot Rot in Goats and Sheep","type":"livestock","affects":"Goats, Sheep, Cattle","severity":"medium","symptoms":"Severe lameness — animals refuse to stand or walk. Painful swelling between hooves. Foul-smelling rot visible between the toes. Hooves deformed in chronic cases. Animals lose weight because they cannot graze normally. Can affect all four legs.","causes":"Bacteria Fusobacterium necrophorum and Dichelobacter nodosus. Enter through damaged skin between hooves. Thrives in wet muddy conditions. Very contagious between animals through contaminated soil.","treatment":"Clean and trim affected hooves. Spray with oxytetracycline spray (Alamycin spray, Terramycin spray). Antibiotic injection — penicillin or oxytetracycline. Zinc sulphate foot bath — walk animals through 10% zinc sulphate solution.","prevention":"Zinc sulphate foot bath monthly — prevention. Keep housing and paddocks dry and clean. Check and trim hooves every 6-8 weeks. Footrot vaccine available where disease is endemic. Quarantine new animals."},

  {"name":"Worms and Internal Parasites","type":"livestock","affects":"All livestock — cattle, goats, sheep, pigs, poultry, rabbits","severity":"high","symptoms":"Loss of weight despite eating well. Pot-bellied appearance especially in young animals. Diarrhea or soft feces. Pale mucous membranes (gums, inner eyelids) — sign of anemia from bloodworms. Rough dull coat. Poor growth rates. Bottle jaw (fluid swelling under jaw) in severe cases in ruminants. Death in severely affected young animals.","causes":"Various roundworms (Haemonchus, Trichostrongylus, Ascaris), tapeworms (Taenia, Moniezia) and flukeworms (Fasciola — liver fluke). Animals pick up larvae by grazing contaminated pastures. Heavy parasite burdens especially after rains when larvae are active on grass.","treatment":"Deworm with appropriate anthelmintic. Cattle/sheep/goats: Levamisole, Albendazole, Ivermectin (Ivomec). Pigs: Ivermectin, Fenbendazole. Poultry: Piperazine, Levamisole in water. Rabbits: Panacur (fenbendazole). Rotate between drug groups to prevent resistance.","prevention":"Deworm all livestock every 3 months — routine prevention. FAMACHA eye test to target treatment to most affected sheep and goats. Pasture management — rotation to break lifecycle. Young animals need more frequent treatment. Avoid overstocking. Liver fluke control: avoid snail habitats (wet areas). Do not put pigs where tapeworm eggs from humans may contaminate (sanitation)."},

  {"name":"Ticks and Tick-Borne Diseases","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle, Sheep, Goats, Dogs","severity":"high","symptoms":"Visible ticks attached to skin especially in folds (armpits, groin, ears, udder, between toes). Heavy tick loads cause blood loss and anemia. Tick worry — animals restless and lose weight. Ticks cause wounds that attract flies and secondary infections. Ticks transmit: ECF, Anaplasmosis, Babesiosis, Heartwater.","causes":"Multiple tick species: Brown Ear Tick (Rhipicephalus appendiculatus — ECF), Blue Tick (Rhipicephalus microplus — Babesia), Bont Tick (Amblyomma variegatum — Heartwater). Ticks pick up disease pathogens from infected animals and transmit to healthy ones.","treatment":"Acaricide dipping or spraying: Triatix (amitraz), Bayticol (flumethrin), Deadline (deltamethrin). Tick removal by hand in small numbers — twist and pull. Treat tick-borne disease specifically (e.g. Butalex for ECF, Berenil for Babesia).","prevention":"Weekly dipping or spraying during tick season — critical. Use acaricide correctly at right concentration. Rotate between different acaricides to prevent resistance. Pasture spelling — rest paddocks for 6+ months to reduce tick numbers. ETT (Ear Tag Treatment) for cattle. Grass management — short grass reduces ticks."},

  {"name":"Mange (Skin Disease)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle, Goats, Sheep, Pigs, Rabbits, Donkeys","severity":"medium","symptoms":"Intense itching — animals rub against posts and fences constantly. Hair loss in patches. Thickened wrinkled crusty skin. Yellow-grey crusts on skin surface. Weight loss from constant irritation. In pigs: affects ears and head. In rabbits: ear mange — dark crusty material in ears.","causes":"Microscopic mites: Sarcoptes scabiei (causes sarcoptic mange — burrowing mite), Psoroptes ovis (psoroptic mange), Demodex species. Spread by direct animal-to-animal contact. Highly contagious in a herd or flock.","treatment":"Inject Ivermectin (Ivomec) — very effective against most mange mites. Two injections 2 weeks apart. Topical treatment with organophosphate dips (Triatix, Tactic). Lime sulfur wash for severe cases. Treat all animals in group simultaneously.","prevention":"Quarantine new animals before introduction. Regular dipping/spraying with acaricide also controls mange mites. Good nutrition supports skin health and resistance. Avoid overcrowding. Regular inspection of all animals."},

  {"name":"Anaplasmosis (Gallsickness)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle","severity":"high","symptoms":"High fever. Severe anemia — pale gums and eyes. Yellow tinge to skin and eyes (jaundice). Difficulty breathing. Weakness and reluctance to move. Dark or bloody urine. Constipation then diarrhea. Death in untreated animals. Surviving animals become lifelong carriers.","causes":"Rickettsial parasite Anaplasma marginale transmitted by ticks (mainly blue tick Rhipicephalus microplus). Also spread by contaminated needles, dehorning equipment and biting flies. More severe in adult cattle than calves.","treatment":"Oxytetracycline (Terramycin LA) injection at full dose — start treatment early. Anti-fever drugs. Blood transfusion in severely anemic animals. Keep animals quiet — excitement causes death in anemic animals.","prevention":"Tick control — dipping or spraying weekly during tick season most important. Calves in tick areas develop natural immunity if exposed young. Avoid moving cattle from low-tick to high-tick areas without vaccination or treatment."},

  {"name":"Contagious Bovine Pleuropneumonia (CBPP)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle only","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Painful cough — dry short painful cough. Difficult breathing — cattle breathe with elbows out and nostrils extended. High fever 40-41°C. Complete loss of appetite and milk production. Animals stand with head lowered. Death in 1-4 weeks. Survivors have permanently damaged lungs.","causes":"Bacteria Mycoplasma mycoides mycoides (small colony type — MmmSC). Spreads by close contact between cattle — inhaling contaminated breath. Spreads rapidly in herds kept together. Can cross borders with cattle movement. Notifiable disease.","treatment":"Oxytetracycline (Terramycin) injection — reduces severity and death rates but treated animals may become chronic carriers. Tylosin and enrofloxacin also used. Isolate sick animals.","prevention":"Annual CBPP vaccination — mandatory in many areas. Report suspected cases to Veterinary Authority immediately — notifiable disease. Avoid mixing cattle from different sources without testing. Quarantine new animals. Movement controls during outbreaks."},

  {"name":"Rabbit Haemorrhagic Disease (RHD)","type":"livestock","affects":"Rabbits only","severity":"critical","symptoms":"Sudden death — rabbits found dead without any warning signs. Or: brief period of fever, loss of appetite, difficulty breathing, paddling movements then death within hours. Blood-stained discharge from nose. Very high mortality 70-100%. Usually affects adult rabbits most severely.","causes":"Rabbit Haemorrhagic Disease Virus (RHDV) type 1 and type 2. Spreads by direct contact, contaminated feed, water, equipment and by flies. Very resistant in environment — survives months. Can spread over large distances.","treatment":"No treatment available. Supportive care may help mild cases. Isolate sick rabbits immediately. Disinfect hutches and equipment thoroughly with 10% bleach solution.","prevention":"Vaccination every 6 months with RHD vaccine — essential and very effective. Biosecurity: prevent wild rabbits from contacting domestic rabbits. Screen hutches to keep out flies. Do not introduce new rabbits without quarantine. Report outbreak to Veterinary Authority."},

  {"name":"Swine Erysipelas","type":"livestock","affects":"Pigs","severity":"high","symptoms":"Sudden high fever. Characteristic diamond-shaped red or purple skin lesions (diamond skin disease) on ears, snout and back. Pigs walk stiffly and are reluctant to move. Sudden deaths in acute cases. In chronic form: swollen painful joints and heart valve damage causing poor performance.","causes":"Bacteria Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae. Lives in soil and intestines of carrier pigs and birds. Outbreaks often follow stress, heat, changes in diet or mixing of pigs. Also affects humans causing skin infection.","treatment":"Penicillin injections — respond very well if treated early. Start treatment immediately when diamond-shaped skin lesions seen. Continue for 5-7 days. Anti-fever drugs for comfort.","prevention":"Vaccination every 6 months. Reduce stress — avoid overcrowding, extreme heat. Good hygiene in pig housing. Provide shade and cooling in hot weather. Clean floors prevent buildup of bacteria."},

  {"name":"Camel Pox","type":"livestock","affects":"Camels — all ages, young most severely","severity":"high","symptoms":"Fever. Skin pustules (pus-filled blisters) on muzzle, lips, nose and around eyes. Pustules dry and form thick scabs. Severe mouth lesions make eating difficult — weight loss. Young camels may die. Adults usually recover but production loss significant.","causes":"Camelpox Virus. Spread by direct contact between camels and by flies. Most severe in young camels and those under nutritional stress. Does not infect other livestock or humans.","treatment":"No specific antiviral treatment. Antibiotic ointment or spray on skin lesions to prevent secondary infection. Anti-fever drugs. Provide soft feeds if mouth lesions prevent eating.","prevention":"Annual vaccination with Camelpox vaccine — effective prevention. Isolate sick animals. Control flies. Good nutrition supports immune system. Avoid overcrowding camels at water points."},

  {"name":"Heartwater (Cowdriosis)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle, Sheep, Goats, Antelope","severity":"critical","symptoms":"High fever 41-42°C. Nervous signs: high-stepping walk. Circling. Head pressing against fence or wall. Convulsions — paddling movements while lying down. Death within 24-48 hours of severe nervous signs. Affected animal cannot eat or drink.","causes":"Ehrlichia (Cowdria) ruminantium — transmitted by Bont tick (Amblyomma variegatum). Tick feeds on infected animal then transmits to next animal bitten. Most severe in exotic or improved breeds. Indigenous breeds more resistant.","treatment":"Tetracycline injection (Terramycin) at very high doses — must start treatment very early in disease. Once nervous signs severe — treatment often too late. Keep animal in quiet dark place to reduce stimulation and convulsions.","prevention":"Tick control with acaricides — weekly dipping or spraying. Remove Bont ticks from animals regularly. Some immunity develops in indigenous breeds exposed young. Avoid moving exotic/grade cattle to tick-infested areas without protection. Heartwater vaccine available in South Africa."},

  {"name":"Trypanosomiasis (Sleeping Sickness/Nagana)","type":"livestock","affects":"Cattle, Camels, Horses, Donkeys, Pigs","severity":"high","symptoms":"Chronic weight loss despite eating. Progressive anemia — pale gums and inner eyelids. Intermittent fever. Enlarged lymph nodes. Watery eyes and nose discharge. Weakness. Reduced milk production and fertility. Eventual death if untreated. Cattle in tsetse fly areas commonly affected.","causes":"Single-celled parasites Trypanosoma brucei, T. congolense, T. vivax transmitted by tsetse fly bite. Also by biting flies (Tabanids) in some areas. Tsetse found in bush, near water, forests and humid areas in western and coastal Kenya.","treatment":"Diminazene aceturate (Berenil, Veriben) injection. Isometamidium chloride (Samorin) — also provides protection for 3-6 months. Treatment must be early for best results. Repeat monthly in high risk areas.","prevention":"Avoid tsetse fly habitat — dense bush and forests near rivers. Clear bush around farms. Tsetse control with traps and targets impregnated with insecticide. Treat cattle with Samorin monthly as preventive in tsetse areas. Pour-on insecticides repel tsetse flies."},
]

def get_all_diseases():
    return DISEASES_DATA
